  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>GQ - Determine the Convergence or Divergence of an Improper Integral</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Evaluate the integral \(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}\,dx\) and determine if it converges or diverges. 
<p>This is improper integral because  [[input:ans1a]] [[validation:ans1a]][[feedback:prt1a]]</p>
<p> So we express the integral as a limit. That is  \(\displaystyle\lim_{t\to 0} \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}\,dx\). Now the integrand is the function [[input:ans1b]] and its antiderivative is given by [[input:ans1c]] [[validation:ans1b]][[validation:ans1c]]</p>
<p> Evaluating the antiderivative at the limits of integration gives </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}\,dx=\)[[input:ans1d]] [[validation:ans1d]][[feedback:prt1b]]</p> 
<p> Finally evaluating the limit as \(t\longrightarrow \infty\) of your answer above gives the value of the integral. (Note: \( \infty\) is entered as <b>inf</b> and \( -\infty\) is entered as <b>minf</b>). </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}\,dx=\lim_{t\to0} \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}\,dx=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>Therefore, the integral is [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>
To evaluate the integral 
\( \displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}  \frac{{@num@}}{{@den1@}{@den2@}} \, dx\),
we first check for Convergence or Divergence of the integral.
</p>
<p>This is an improper integral because The limits of integration contain a point of discontinuity, The integrand is not continuous at a point in the interval of integration and The integrand is not defined a point in the interval of integration</p>
<p>So we express the integral as a limit. That is  \(\displaystyle\lim_{t\to\infty} \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}\,dx\). Now the integrand is the function\(\displaystyle {@ta_1b@}\)  and its antiderivative is given by \(\displaystyle{@ta_1c@}\)</p>
<p> Evaluating the antiderivative at the limits of integration gives </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}\,dx={@ta_1d@}\)</p> 
<p> Finally evaluating the limit as \(t\longrightarrow \infty\) of your answer above gives the value of the integral. (Note: \( \infty\) is entered as <b>inf</b> and \( -\infty\) is entered as <b>minf</b>). </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}\,dx=\lim_{t\to\infty} \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}\,dx={@ta_1@}\)</p>
<p>
Now, we integrate \( \displaystyle {@func@}\,dx\) with respect to \(x\):</p>
<p> \( \qquad \displaystyle  \begin{align} \int {@func@}\,dx &= {@num@} \cdot \frac{x^{{@-power@} + 1}}{{@-power@} + 1}  \\
                                                  & = {@num@} \cdot \frac{x^{{@-power+1@}}}{ \displaystyle {@-power+1@}} \\
                                                  &= {@new_indef@}
   \end{align} \)</p>

<p>
Thus,
\(\displaystyle  \displaystyle  \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}  {@func@}\,dx = \lim_{t \to {@lower_limit@}^{+}} \left[ {@new_indef@} \right]_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}\)
</p>

<p>
Substitute the limits:
\(\displaystyle  \lim_{t \to {@lower_limit@}^{+}} \left( \displaystyle {@ta@} \right) \to {@ta_1@}\). 
<p>\( \implies \displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}  {@func@}\,dx \to {@ta_1@}\).
</p>

<p>Therefore, the integral is \(\displaystyle  \text{@ta_2@}\) </p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
